# The number of triples in the OP source graphs, to check the read against.
# @PATTERNS@ is one GRAPH <...> { ?s ?p ?o } per source graph, written by
# export.sh.
#
# @SKIP@ carries the same excluded-subject filter as export-page.rq, so the
# expected count is over exactly the subjects the pages read. Both are written
# by export.sh and must stay in step.
#
# The UNION over the source graphs can yield the same triple twice when it sits
# in two of them (469 triples do, in the dev data), so the count is over distinct
# triples, not over union rows. A plain count over the union read high by 469 and
# the export refused a complete read; the load graph, which is a set, had the
# distinct number. SELECT DISTINCT in the sub-select is what makes COUNT(*) the
# distinct count here -- COUNT(DISTINCT ?s, ?p, ?o) is not usable on this store.
SELECT (COUNT(*) AS ?c) WHERE {
  { SELECT DISTINCT ?s ?p ?o WHERE { @PATTERNS@ FILTER(@SKIP@) } }
}
